// Practice quiz: Fetching data
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/fetch-and-browser-client/fetching-data

::o1 two steps::Why does reading JSON from fetch usually need two awaits? {
=The response arrives with its headers first, and reading the body is a second asynchronous step #Correct. res.json() returns its own promise.
~fetch sends the request twice #Not quite. It sends it once.
~JSON is always parsed on the server #Not quite. Parsing happens in the browser here.
~The first await only checks the network #Not quite. The first await gives you the Response.
}

::o1 body once::You call await res.json() and then call it again on the same response. What happens? {
=The second call rejects, because the body was already read #Correct. A body stream can be consumed only once.
~It returns the same object again #Not quite. The stream cannot be replayed without a clone.
~It sends a new request #Not quite. Reading a body does not send anything.
~It returns an empty object #Not quite. It rejects rather than returning.
}

::o2 when it rejects::Which situation makes the promise from fetch reject? {
=The network fails and no response arrives #Correct. Rejection means no HTTP response was received.
~The server answers 500 #Not quite. That is a response, so it fulfills.
~The server answers 404 #Not quite. That is a response, so it fulfills.
~The body is not JSON #Not quite. That rejects res.json(), not fetch.
}

::o2 ok range::For which status is res.ok true? {
=204 #Correct. ok is true for statuses 200 through 299.
~304 #Not quite. That is outside the range.
~404 #Not quite. That is a client error.
~500 #Not quite. That is a server error.
}

::o3 safe render::A title from your own API is displayed in a list. Which is the safe approach? {
=Create an li and set its textContent to the title #Correct. The title stays plain text.
~Join titles into an HTML string for innerHTML, since it is your own API #Not quite. Your API can still return text that users wrote.
~Wrap the title in a template literal #Not quite. A template literal does not escape anything.
~Trim the title first #Not quite. Trimming removes spaces, not markup.
}
